    68	### Capped-gain mechanism decomposition
    69	
    70	The decomposition is additive in percentage points relative to C1 capped EE. The energy-only term holds the capped numerator at C1 and changes only the denominator: `E1/E2 − 1`. The extra-credited-bits term is the exact residual evaluated at C2 energy: `(B2−B1)/E2 ÷ (B1/E1)`. Their sum is the total capped gain.
    71	
    72	| Rule | Total capped gain | Energy-only component | Share of total | Extra credited-bits component | Share of total |
    73	|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
    74	| `SEALED` | +82.7829% | +27.9796% | 33.80% | +54.8033% | 66.20% |
    75	| `STRICT` | +1.9348% | +0.5736% | 29.65% | +1.3611% | 70.35% |
    76	
    77	## Derived on paper
    78	
    79	I agree with the proposed implication as a real-arithmetic derivation: if every user-anchor has `C2 delivery ≥ C1 delivery`, monotonicity of `min(x, 1.504 Gbit)` makes the pooled capped numerator nondecreasing, hence capped `EE2/EE1 − 1 ≥ E1/E2 − 1`.
    80	
    81	I do **not** confirm the premise literally in the replayed binary64 receipts: the two violations above are each −2.384185791015625e−7 bit. Therefore +27.9796% and +0.5736% are conditional energy-only bounds from the stated real-arithmetic invariant, not strict guarantees from every replayed floating row. Direct measurement independently shows that both pooled capped numerators increase substantially, so the actual gains exceed those energy-only values.
    82	
    83	Using full-precision receipt energies:
    84	
    85	| Rule | C1 energy (kJ) | C2 energy (kJ) | Conditional energy-only bound | Actual capped gain |
    86	|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
    87	| `SEALED` | 21.895529 | 17.108606 | +27.9796% | +82.7829% |
    88	| `STRICT` | 29.227048 | 29.060348 | +0.5736% | +1.9348% |
    89	
    90	## Inferred
    91	
    92	The raw-capacity headline is numerator-sensitive. The capped result preserves the denominator benefit and credits only delivery up to each user's contracted endpoint demand. `ATTAINMENT_ONLY` answers a different question and has an undefined SEALED relative gain because its C1 numerator is zero. These are fixed-panel diagnostics, not learner-recoverable or population claims. Loss values were not used as evidence about EE.
    93	
    94	The two −2.384185791015625e−7-bit invariant violations are inferred to be floating-operation-order artifacts: the C1 path multiplies `fraction × bandwidth × spectral efficiency`, whereas the C2 replay path forms `bandwidth × spectral efficiency` before multiplying by the fraction. Their size and the passing aggregate parity support that inference, but they are still reported as literal violations and were not rounded away for the proof.
    95	
    96	The current sibling replay script does not match the script hash printed in the source report. The sealed JSON does match its printed hash, all pinned executable dependencies pass their own hashes, and both C1 and C2 numerical replay parity pass; it is therefore reasonable—but explicitly an inference—to attribute the current script drift to later diagnostic plumbing rather than a numerically material evaluator change.
    97	
    98	## Owed repairs (no sealed artefact change proposed)
    99	
   100	- Future control-law-headroom reports should name the numerator in the headline and present demand-capped results beside raw capacity.
   101	
   102	- Future receipts should serialize per-user integrated endpoint delivery and the selected C2 schedule directly, so a sibling-workspace replay is unnecessary.
   103	
